# Village weaver

The village weaver is a common, gregarious weaver of sub-Saharan Africa, the
breeding male black-faced and bright yellow. The male strips fibres from palm
fronds and grasses and plaits them into a roofed, kidney-shaped chamber with a
downward entrance, suspended from the tip of a drooping branch over a road or a
watercourse. He may complete a dozen such chambers in a season, displaying
beneath each as a female arrives to inspect it. Colonies are noisy, and a single
tree may carry two hundred pendulous chambers. See [[ploceidae]].
